I'll verify that the ring of $p$-adic integers $O_p = \mathbb{Z}_p$ is open in $\mathbb{Q}_p$.

**Setup.** Let $v_p : \mathbb{Q}_p \to \mathbb{Z} \cup \{\infty\}$ be the $p$-adic valuation and $|\cdot|_p : \mathbb{Q}_p \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$ the $p$-adic absolute value, $|x|_p = p^{-v_p(x)}$ (with $|0|_p = 0$). This is a non-Archimedean absolute value, so it satisfies the **ultrametric inequality**:
$$|x + y|_p \le \max\bigl(|x|_p,\, |y|_p\bigr).$$

The ring of $p$-adic integers is
$$O_p = \mathbb{Z}_p = \{x \in \mathbb{Q}_p : v_p(x) \ge 0\} = \{x \in \mathbb{Q}_p : |x|_p \le 1\},$$
i.e. the closed unit ball about the origin.

**Goal.** Show $\mathbb{Z}_p$ is open in the topology on $\mathbb{Q}_p$ induced by $|\cdot|_p$.

**Proof.** It suffices to show that every point of $\mathbb{Z}_p$ is an interior point, i.e. for each $x \in \mathbb{Z}_p$ there exists an open ball $B(x, r) \subseteq \mathbb{Z}_p$.

Fix $x \in \mathbb{Z}_p$, so $|x|_p \le 1$. Consider the open ball of radius $1$ about $x$:
$$B(x, 1) = \{y \in \mathbb{Q}_p : |y - x|_p < 1\}.$$

For any $y \in B(x, 1)$, the ultrametric inequality gives
$$|y|_p = |x + (y - x)|_p \le \max\bigl(|x|_p,\, |y - x|_p\bigr).$$

Since $x \in \mathbb{Z}_p$ we have $|x|_p \le 1$, and since $y \in B(x,1)$ we have $|y - x|_p < 1 \le 1$. Hence
$$|y|_p \le \max\bigl(\le 1,\, <1\bigr) \le 1.$$

Therefore $y \in \mathbb{Z}_p$. This shows $B(x, 1) \subseteq \mathbb{Z}_p$.

Since $x \in \mathbb{Z}_p$ was arbitrary, every point of $\mathbb{Z}_p$ is interior, so
$$\mathbb{Z}_p = \bigcup_{x \in \mathbb{Z}_p} B(x, 1)$$
is a union of open balls and is therefore open. $\blacksquare$

**Remark.** This is a characteristic feature of non-Archimedean geometry: in an ultrametric space every point of a closed ball is a center, and closed balls of radius $r$ are simultaneously open (being unions of open balls of any radius $< r$... in fact here the closed ball $B(0,1)$ equals the union of open balls $B(x,1)$ for $x$ ranging over it). In particular $\mathbb{Z}_p$ is both closed *and* open (clopen) in $\mathbb{Q}_p$. The same argument shows that for every $n \in \mathbb{Z}$, the "annulus" $\{x : v_p(x) \ge n\} = p^n \mathbb{Z}_p$ is clopen, and these form a neighborhood basis of $0$ consisting of open subgroups.

### PROOF COMPLETE
